# TuningFork in a notebook

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tuning_fork, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_samples, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_midi, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_kino, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## One tune, three ways

A tune in D minor at 100 bpm: kick, snare and hats from a Roland TR-909, a fingered bass,
electric piano stabs on Dm, B♭, F and C, a flute line over the top and strings underneath.
The three cells below are it written three ways, so the spellings can be compared with the
sound held constant: the same recordings, the same tempo, and the same arrangement —
strings and chords alone, then drums and bass, the flute over them, a breakdown with a
second flute line, then everything with an arpeggio on top — about seventy seconds before
it comes round again. Press **▶ listen** on a cell's stage once, then play.

## Compose

A step sequencer you click. Each row is a track — a drum, an instrument or a recording — and
each cell a step: a number is a scale degree, a longer cell a held note. **▶ play** loops the
grid live on a stage of the cell's own, repeat by repeat — an edit lands at the next repeat,
as in the two live cells below, and the readout says which repeat is sounding — and the cell
writes the same music out as `TuningFork` source underneath, so a grid becomes code you can
keep editing by hand. The grid is eight beats of four steps — two bars
— and **Bars** is 14, so the piece is the grid fourteen times over; the text box on each
track is which of those repeats it plays, `x` for on and `.` for off, which is where the
arrangement lives: the kick reads `..xxxxxx..xxxx`, the flute `....xxxx..xxxx`, the
breakdown flute `........xx....`. **Kit** picks the sounds: `RolandTR909` here, so the drums
are that machine's recordings and the instruments General MIDI soundfonts, the same ones the
two cells below play; `synth` is the kit's own drums and synthesised instruments, with
nothing to fetch.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.ComposerCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
import TuningFork.Part

alias TuningFork.{Kit, Score, Voice}

base =
  Voice.new(
    shape: :saw,
    gain: 0.5,
    cutoff: 0.45,
    envelope: TuningFork.Envelope.new(attack: 0.006, sustain: 0.3, release: 0.12)
  )

bass = Kit.instrument("gm_electric_bass_finger", 0.5, %{gain: 0.5})
epiano = Kit.instrument("gm_epiano1", 0.5, %{gain: 0.5})
pipe = Kit.instrument("gm_flute", 0.5, %{gain: 0.5})
strings = Kit.instrument("gm_string_ensemble_1", 0.5, %{gain: 0.5})

drum_kick =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: Kit.instrument("bd", 0.5, %{bank: "RolandTR909", gain: 0.5}), gain: 0.9)
  |> repeat("..xxxxxx..xxxx", fn bar -> steps(bar, "x.......x.x.....x.......x.x..x..", 1 / 4) end)

drum_snare =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: Kit.instrument("sd", 0.5, %{bank: "RolandTR909", gain: 0.5}), gain: 0.6)
  |> repeat("..xxxxxx..xxxx", fn bar -> steps(bar, "....x.......x.......x.......x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_clap =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: Kit.instrument("cp", 0.5, %{bank: "RolandTR909", gain: 0.5}), gain: 0.4)
  |> repeat("..xxxxxx..xxxx", fn bar -> steps(bar, "............x...............x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_hat =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: Kit.instrument("hh", 0.5, %{bank: "RolandTR909", gain: 0.5}), gain: 0.35)
  |> repeat(".xxxxxxxxxxxxx", fn bar -> steps(bar, "x.x.x.x.x.x.x...x.x.x.x.x.x.x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_open_hat =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: Kit.instrument("oh", 0.5, %{bank: "RolandTR909", gain: 0.5}), gain: 0.3)
  |> repeat(".xxxxxxxxxxxxx", fn bar -> steps(bar, "..............x...............x.", 1 / 4) end)

pitched_bass =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: bass, gain: 0.6)
  |> repeat("..xxxxxxxxxxxx", fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:d2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :d2,
        {:d2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :a2,
        nil,
        {:as2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :as2,
        {:as2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :f2,
        nil,
        {:f2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :f2,
        {:f2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :c3,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :c3,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :g2,
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_epiano =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: epiano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat("nilnilnilnilni", fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:d3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_epiano_2 =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: epiano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat("nilnilnilnilni", fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:e3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:e3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_epiano_3 =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: epiano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat("nilnilnilnilni", fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:a3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:a3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:as3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
